# Spec: Simulator of Curved 3D Space on the Surface of 4D Shapes

## 1. Task

A browser-based HTML/JS application: the user moves freely through three-dimensional space — the intrinsic geometry of a hypersurface (a 3-manifold) bounding a 4-dimensional shape in R⁴. The curvature is the result of computations on the 4D representation, not a visual imitation.

## 2. Representation and Movement

- The world is stored and computed as an object in R⁴.
- The observer's position and orientation are a point and a local tangent frame on the manifold.
- Movement transports the observer's entire frame (forward, "up," "right") along the manifold via parallel transport, not by recomputation using a global rule.
- Both classes of hypersurfaces are required:
  - smooth (C², curvature defined at every point via the curvature tensor of the induced metric, geodesics as the solution of a differential equation);
  - piecewise-flat (the boundary of a 4-dimensional polytope: flat metric within cells, curvature concentrated as an angular defect on a singular submanifold, geodesics continue into the adjacent cell upon crossing a face).
- Forbidden: shader-based distortion layered over Euclidean geometry; fisheye/cubemap skybox effects while the camera moves along a flat trajectory; manipulation limited to the projection matrix/FOV; any method where the actual coordinates remain flat 3D and the curvature is merely an on-screen effect.

## 3. Light and Rendering

Rays propagate along geodesics of the induced metric (in piecewise-flat scenes — continuing across faces). Visual distortions are a direct result of this computation.

## 4. Scenes

- At least 4 scenes: one smooth with positive curvature, one smooth with non-positive/mixed-sign curvature, and at least one piecewise-flat scene with non-trivial topology (not simply connected and/or featuring "exit on one side, reappear on the other").
- Each scene must contain visually distinguishable landmarks for judging distance and path curvature. A single repeating primitive (stripes, dots, a grid) as the entire visual content of a scene is not allowed.

